}
```

`lookupValues` always has at least one entry because `{ type: "all", value: "*" }` is unconditionally pushed at line 113 before this check. The guard can never trigger. This means a request with no `productId`, no `collectionId`, and no `chartId` will silently fall through to a DB query that scans all `assignmentType = "all"` rows for the shop — potentially returning a chart intended for "all products" even when the merchant only wanted a chart for a specific product. The missing-params branch should return an error early.

**Fix:** Move the `all` wildcard push to after the early-return check:
```tsx
// lines 104-120 — reorder so the guard can fire
const lookupValues: Array<{ type: string; value: string }> = [];
if (productId) lookupValues.push({ type: "product", value: productId });
if (collectionId) lookupValues.push({ type: "collection", value: collectionId });

if (!chartId && lookupValues.length === 0) {
  return Response.json(
    { error: "At least one of chartId, productId, or collectionId is required" },
    { status: 400, headers: CORS_HEADERS },
  );
}
// Always include the wildcard "all" fallback
lookupValues.push({ type: "all", value: "*" });
```

